## The process is the protagonist

Our *Impact of AI on work* engagement had two phases. In Phase 1, any Californian could share their views through an open online survey. For Phase 2, we invited a smaller group of those who responded to live, moderated discussions. We needed a way to decide who to invite that was fair to all respondents.

To choose those participants, we used an approach called sortition, which is similar to how juries are chosen. It is built on two equally important ideas.

The first is **representation**. We set demographic goals based on California population estimates. This would make the final group reflect the diversity of the state across things like age, geography, and field of work.

The second is **randomness**. Within those goals, everyone who fit had an equal chance of being selected.

Together, these principles ensured our deeper conversations weren't shaped only by those who happened to respond first. It let us build a group that was fairly chosen and roughly reflective of Californians as a whole.

----
id: process_links
----

**Links**

- Our sortition code: [https://github.com/cagov/caldata-engaged-california/blob/main/notebooks/AI%20Engagement%20-%20Sortition/ai_engagement_sortition.ipynb](https://github.com/cagov/caldata-engaged-california/blob/main/notebooks/AI%20Engagement%20-%20Sortition/ai_engagement_sortition.ipynb)
- Sortition Foundation “Why sortition algorithms” [https://sortitionfoundation.github.io/sortition-algorithms/animated-intro.html](https://sortitionfoundation.github.io/sortition-algorithms/animated-intro.html)

**Sources for each category targets**

- Geography: *Rationale: Every region gets a guaranteed voice (Min 6), with the remaining seats filled proportionally by California's 2026 population density using American Community Survey (ACS) estimates.*
- Industry: *Rationale: Based on California Employment Development Department’s Industry Employment & Labor Force (CES) statistics (for April 2026).* The memo to the Governor’s Office specifically calls out logistics, healthcare, legal and financial services, creative industries, the public sector, and academia as priority sectors, reflected in categories below. [https://labormarketinfo.edd.ca.gov/file/lfmonth/cal$pds.pdf](https://labormarketinfo.edd.ca.gov/file/lfmonth/cal$pds.pdf) [https://labormarketinfo.edd.ca.gov/cgi/dataanalysis/areaselection.asp?tablename=ces](https://labormarketinfo.edd.ca.gov/cgi/dataanalysis/areaselection.asp?tablename=ces)
- Age: *Rationale: Mirrors 2026 Census/ CA DOF projections.*
- Gender: *Rationale: Mirrors 2026 Census/ CA DOF projections.*
- Race / Ethnicity: *Rationale: Mirrors 2026 Census/ CA DOF projections.*

